WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.720
Well, I want to talk today about the relative orientation of the camera pair, the fundamental

00:08.720 --> 00:10.680
matrix and the central matrix.

00:10.680 --> 00:16.960
So we are now moving on from using a single camera to using two cameras.

00:16.960 --> 00:19.440
How do two camera systems look like?

00:19.440 --> 00:23.160
So this here is an example for a camera pair.

00:23.160 --> 00:26.120
So this is a standard stereo camera.

00:26.120 --> 00:30.640
That means we have two cameras, two physical cameras which are rigidly attached to each

00:30.640 --> 00:37.640
other and take images at the same point in time so that we can work with pairs of images

00:37.640 --> 00:39.720
taken from different locations.

00:39.720 --> 00:45.080
And this is an example for a so-called stereo normal case where both cameras are looking

00:45.080 --> 00:46.080
in the right direction.

00:46.080 --> 00:52.720
We just have an offset along our one direction of our two images.

00:52.720 --> 00:55.400
But that's not the only example for a camera pair.

00:55.400 --> 00:59.760
It can only be just one physical camera which is moving through the environment.

00:59.760 --> 01:04.800
So I take one image from here, move my camera over here and take a second image from the

01:04.800 --> 01:05.800
other side.

01:05.800 --> 01:10.800
This would also be something that we would qualify as a camera pair and for which we

01:10.800 --> 01:12.600
want to do estimation.

01:12.600 --> 01:17.000
And so just to remind you of that, a camera pair is not just a stereo camera.

01:17.000 --> 01:20.960
It can be also one camera that moves through the environment or two cameras which move

01:20.960 --> 01:22.580
freely through the scene.

01:22.580 --> 01:27.540
So when we talk about a camera pair, we are basically talking about two configurations

01:27.540 --> 01:32.920
from which images have been taken and we are using those two images to estimate something.

01:32.920 --> 01:39.340
For example, what is the relative orientation of my two cameras when they were taking their

01:39.340 --> 01:40.780
images.

01:40.780 --> 01:44.720
And in the lecture day, we want to look into the relative orientation, what can we say

01:44.720 --> 01:52.100
about the relative orientation and then look into two very essential matrices, the so-called

01:52.620 --> 01:57.500
fundamental matrix and the essential matrix which are two matrices, one for the uncalibrated

01:57.500 --> 02:04.060
camera and one for the calibrated camera to describe the relative orientation among those

02:04.060 --> 02:05.800
cameras.

02:05.800 --> 02:09.940
So we start with describing the relative orientation of the camera pair.

02:09.940 --> 02:16.500
So how can we describe the orientation in an absolute manner and in a relative manner.

02:16.500 --> 02:21.860
And so when we think about orientation, so what are the orientation parameters in a camera.

02:21.860 --> 02:28.140
And these are the parameters which are involved in our mapping x equals px, something that

02:28.140 --> 02:33.340
we have done for the single camera case when we were describing the mapping, how a point

02:33.340 --> 02:37.340
from the 3D bolt is mapped onto my camera image.

02:37.340 --> 02:43.220
And the question now, if I have two cameras or camera pair, either two fixed mounted cameras

02:43.220 --> 02:49.840
or freely moving camera, how many parameters do I actually need to describe that mapping?

02:49.840 --> 02:53.520
So how many free parameters do I have?

02:53.520 --> 02:56.600
You can think about this, how does it work for a calibrated camera?

02:56.600 --> 03:01.800
That means a camera where my calibration matrix is known and it's already applied so that

03:01.800 --> 03:06.960
I can directly measure my points in my camera coordinate system.

03:06.960 --> 03:12.100
Or how would that look like for an uncalibrated camera where I don't know my calibration matrix?

03:12.100 --> 03:14.160
So how many parameters do I need?

03:14.160 --> 03:20.080
So if I have a calibrated camera, the only free parameters are the orientation of each

03:20.080 --> 03:25.280
camera, so where is it looking to, the heading information, as well as the position information.

03:25.280 --> 03:32.600
So where is the projection center of every camera?

03:32.600 --> 03:38.200
So this is three for the translation, three for the rotation for the first camera, and

03:38.200 --> 03:42.600
then three more for the rotation and for the translation for the second camera.

03:42.600 --> 03:45.940
So we have three plus three plus three plus three.

03:45.940 --> 03:52.920
So we have 12 parameters for the calibrated camera, which are two poses, two position

03:52.920 --> 03:54.980
and heading information.

03:54.980 --> 04:02.080
For the uncalibrated camera, we can assume that, or we may not be able to assume that

04:02.080 --> 04:08.600
the camera has the same calibration matrix because it could be two cameras or like this,

04:08.600 --> 04:11.940
the stereo pair and both may have a different calibration matrix.

04:11.940 --> 04:16.240
So again, if you remember, the calibration matrix had five parameters, so that means

04:16.240 --> 04:21.440
we have 10 more parameters for the uncalibrated case because we have two cameras, each camera

04:21.440 --> 04:28.060
has five additional parameters, so it's 12 plus 10, so 22 parameters.

04:28.060 --> 04:34.160
The first case, we have 12 parameters, six plus six, and here we have 11 plus 11 parameters

04:34.160 --> 04:40.880
that we need in order to describe the parameters of the mapping that is generated by the camera

04:40.880 --> 04:41.880
up here.

04:41.880 --> 04:48.840
So 12 and 22 parameters is the theoretical maximum of parameters that we can use to describe

04:48.840 --> 04:50.100
the camera.

04:50.100 --> 04:55.760
So the key question is, can we actually estimate camera motion without knowing the scene?

04:55.760 --> 05:00.960
It's kind of the first key question that we want to answer.

05:00.960 --> 05:06.680
So if you think about the single camera case, we exploited here knowledge about the scene

05:06.680 --> 05:09.200
in order to describe where a camera is.

05:09.200 --> 05:14.320
So if you think about the projective three-point algorithm, there we used three control points

05:14.320 --> 05:21.320
in the environment in order to estimate the position and orientation of one single camera.

05:21.320 --> 05:27.000
And if you think about the uncalibrated camera, we had the DLT where we needed five control

05:27.000 --> 05:30.500
points to estimate the 11 parameters for one camera.

05:30.500 --> 05:33.200
But all this exploited knowledge about the scene.

05:33.200 --> 05:37.280
We were assuming we know point in the environment.

05:37.280 --> 05:42.280
And here we want to go a step further and ignore what we know, that we know anything

05:42.280 --> 05:43.440
about the environment.

05:43.440 --> 05:47.780
So we do not want to know specific location of three points in the world.

05:47.780 --> 05:51.600
We just want to work based on the images that the two cameras provide us.

05:51.600 --> 05:57.040
So either we have two images, one from camera one, one from camera two, or we have two images,

05:57.040 --> 06:00.840
one taken from the camera at point one, camera moving through the environment to a different

06:00.840 --> 06:04.160
location taking a second image, and we have those second images.

06:04.160 --> 06:09.520
And the question is, can we describe the movement of that camera in the scene?

06:09.520 --> 06:11.600
This is one of the key questions.

06:11.600 --> 06:17.000
And for that, we first need to answer which parameters can we actually obtain if we don't

06:17.000 --> 06:21.240
have any knowledge about the scene and which parameters we may not be able to estimate

06:21.240 --> 06:24.840
at all from our camera.

06:24.840 --> 06:33.280
And one thing that you may have observed, it's hard to estimate scale from camera information.

06:33.280 --> 06:38.080
So if you have one single image of an object, it's hard to say how large the object actually

06:38.080 --> 06:39.080
is.

06:39.080 --> 06:43.200
So we as humans often can estimate the size, but this is basically something that we have

06:43.200 --> 06:44.200
learned.

06:44.200 --> 06:49.360
So our system knows how large certain objects are and then assumes a certain size or relates

06:49.360 --> 06:51.300
different people with each other.

06:51.300 --> 06:57.240
But it's actually from the images itself impossible, without having any information about the scene,

06:57.240 --> 07:01.520
to estimate the size of the object that we are looking onto.

07:01.800 --> 07:05.880
So you can even create images which generates a false impression for us humans, where you

07:05.880 --> 07:09.320
have objects which are typically very small and just scale them up.

07:09.320 --> 07:10.320
And it's very hard.

07:10.320 --> 07:14.620
You can easily confuse people that they are assuming the size of the objects to be wrong.

07:14.620 --> 07:21.080
So we already know that from a single image, scale is something that we cannot easily estimate.

07:21.080 --> 07:25.720
And this also depends from the fact that we don't know how far the objects are away, that

07:25.720 --> 07:30.400
the camera is basically a direction measurement device which tells us in which direction,

07:30.400 --> 07:33.160
which light intensity has been measured.

07:33.160 --> 07:40.280
And it is actually similar for the case that I have two cameras observing the same scene.

07:40.280 --> 07:44.080
There are certain parameters which I cannot estimate.

07:44.080 --> 07:50.900
And there's actually one parameter for the calibrated case that is hard for us to estimate

07:50.900 --> 07:54.320
if you just think about the relative orientation of the camera.

07:54.320 --> 07:56.940
And so we can nicely describe it with this figure.

07:56.940 --> 08:00.940
So all the variables which have a single prime refer to camera number one.

08:00.940 --> 08:06.200
And all the variables with two primes refer to camera number two.

08:06.200 --> 08:08.240
So this is the location of camera number one.

08:08.240 --> 08:09.740
And I just fix it for now.

08:09.740 --> 08:12.060
Let's say this be the center of the reference frame.

08:12.060 --> 08:14.580
So I'm not caring about where this camera is globally.

08:14.580 --> 08:21.060
I'm just looking here saying I want to express everything with respect to camera number one.

08:21.060 --> 08:25.140
And then I can place camera number two, for example, over here looking into this direction

08:25.140 --> 08:27.320
over there.

08:27.320 --> 08:28.320
And then we see a box.

08:28.320 --> 08:31.540
Let's say this is the box that we are seeing, but we have no idea about the size of the

08:31.540 --> 08:34.180
box or even the knowledge that it is a box.

08:34.180 --> 08:39.540
And so if we have a point x over here, there's a certain ray in our camera image number one

08:39.540 --> 08:40.540
where we see this point.

08:40.540 --> 08:46.180
There's a certain ray in camera image number two where we see that point.

08:46.180 --> 08:51.580
And if we would have perfect knowledge about the scene, that would be sufficient to allow

08:51.580 --> 08:56.280
us to estimate where that camera number two actually is.

08:56.280 --> 08:59.540
The problem that we have, however, is that we do not know anything about the scene.

08:59.540 --> 09:03.180
We wanted a grid of the assumption that we know what we are perceiving.

09:03.180 --> 09:09.340
So what we can do now is we can shift actually the camera along this line to this position

09:09.340 --> 09:15.980
and then observe the object so that the same ray, the same pixel that has seen this object

09:15.980 --> 09:20.100
here, still sees the object over here.

09:20.100 --> 09:23.760
But the object is simply larger.

09:23.760 --> 09:29.060
So by moving the camera further away, we can actually generate the same physical image

09:29.060 --> 09:32.820
if we would scale up the size of the scene.

09:32.820 --> 09:39.820
And this is an important insight that we cannot obtain the full translation and rotation of

09:39.820 --> 09:43.420
a second camera with respect to a first camera.

09:43.420 --> 09:50.380
Because if we move the camera along the scene, we could generate exactly the same image just

09:50.380 --> 09:53.020
by scaling up and down the scene.

09:53.020 --> 09:56.740
And either we know something about the scene, such as a scale, then we can actually nail

09:56.740 --> 09:57.900
it down.

09:57.900 --> 10:03.740
But if we don't know anything about the scene, it's impossible for us to say where the second

10:03.740 --> 10:09.480
camera is located along this line over here.

10:09.480 --> 10:17.000
So this means we are not able to estimate the global, the scale, so how far the second

10:17.000 --> 10:19.360
camera is away from the first camera.

10:19.360 --> 10:24.800
And something that is obvious that we also do not know is where this camera number one

10:24.800 --> 10:25.900
is located.

10:25.900 --> 10:29.720
So in the explanation so far, I just said, let's fix this.

10:29.720 --> 10:33.620
And as a center of the reference frame, express everything with respect to this camera.

10:33.620 --> 10:35.580
Then I'm missing the scale information.

10:35.580 --> 10:39.160
But what I also do not know is where are those objects in the scene or where is this

10:39.160 --> 10:41.240
camera in a global reference frame.

10:41.240 --> 10:45.100
So the global translation and global rotation is kind of lost.

10:45.100 --> 10:49.960
But I can actually express everything related to the camera.

10:49.960 --> 10:54.660
But we'll know the opposite thing, what I can actually estimate of my 12 parameters.

10:54.660 --> 10:58.920
So again, over all these 12 parameters, it seems the global translation and the global

10:58.920 --> 11:00.720
rotation I cannot estimate.

11:00.720 --> 11:04.900
So three of those parameters, six of these parameters are already gone.

11:04.900 --> 11:07.800
Three for the translation, three for the rotation.

11:07.800 --> 11:10.400
And I can't resolve the scale information.

11:10.400 --> 11:16.080
So where I am along this line, there's again, one parameter I cannot estimate.

11:16.080 --> 11:22.720
So from these 12 parameters, it turns out that seven parameters I can actually not estimate.

11:22.720 --> 11:27.560
What I, however, will be able to do is estimate the remaining five parameters.

11:27.560 --> 11:32.080
Where is the second camera with respect to the first camera, except of the scale information,

11:32.080 --> 11:35.080
this is something I also cannot resolve.

11:35.080 --> 11:39.800
So what we can do is, we can estimate the rotation R of the second camera with respect

11:39.800 --> 11:41.200
to the first camera.

11:41.200 --> 11:45.100
This gives us a rotation matrix and these three parameters I can fix.

11:45.100 --> 11:50.960
What I can also fix is the direction of this solid line I've drawn over here.

11:50.960 --> 11:55.180
And this basically line on which the second camera can slide.

11:55.180 --> 11:59.440
And this is basically a direction vector B connecting the projection centers.

11:59.440 --> 12:03.560
The direction of this vector I can estimate, so two parameters are fixed.

12:03.560 --> 12:09.240
What I do not know is the length of this vector, the distance that those cameras have.

12:09.240 --> 12:13.680
So this, I don't know how far it is away, I only know the direction along which the

12:13.680 --> 12:16.520
second camera is being located.

12:16.520 --> 12:23.240
And this again is directly related to the scale of the scene and the orientation of

12:23.240 --> 12:24.820
the first camera.

12:24.820 --> 12:28.400
So the global scale rotation is because I have no knowledge about the scene, I do not

12:28.400 --> 12:32.000
know where those objects are in the world.

12:32.000 --> 12:36.840
So to summarize this, for my calibrated camera, I would need two times six equals to twelve

12:36.840 --> 12:43.300
parameters to describe the orientation of the camera up here.

12:43.300 --> 12:50.400
So with a calibrated camera, we obtain so-called angle preserving mapping or angle preserving

12:50.400 --> 12:52.900
model of our object.

12:52.900 --> 13:00.560
So without further information about the scene, we can only estimate twelve minus seven equals

13:00.560 --> 13:02.360
to five parameters.

13:02.360 --> 13:06.960
What we are not able to obtain are the seven parameters, translation rotation of the scene

13:06.960 --> 13:12.200
or of the first camera and the distance between the two cameras.

13:12.200 --> 13:19.340
So this is something that I cannot obtain, I only can obtain these five parameters.

13:19.340 --> 13:24.060
So there's basically a similarity transform, three translation parameters, three rotation

13:24.060 --> 13:28.840
parameters, one scale parameter that I actually cannot resolve.

13:28.840 --> 13:34.720
This holds for the calibrated camera so I know the calibration matrix.

13:34.720 --> 13:42.260
And the resulting model that I get if I build a model of a scene giving these two calibrated

13:42.260 --> 13:46.300
cameras is so-called photogrammetric model.

13:46.300 --> 13:51.440
And this is a 3D model of the scene but which is only known up to a similarity transform.

13:51.440 --> 13:56.400
So I'm missing the global rotation and translation and I'm missing the size of the object, so

13:56.400 --> 13:58.800
the scale of the object is not available.

13:58.800 --> 14:03.040
And that's something that we refer to as the so-called photogrammetric model.

14:03.040 --> 14:09.520
If I want to put the photogrammetric model into the real world, so that means fixing

14:09.520 --> 14:14.600
this similarity transform, the scale and where it is in the real world, this is then something

14:14.600 --> 14:19.280
which I call computing the absolute orientation.

14:19.280 --> 14:22.640
This I have to distinguish from the relative orientation.

14:22.640 --> 14:25.880
So in this lecture here we'll talk a lot about the relative orientation.

14:25.880 --> 14:31.200
Here's camera two with respect to camera one in terms of these five parameters for

14:31.200 --> 14:34.160
the calibrated camera that I can actually estimate.

14:34.160 --> 14:38.640
If I then want to do an absolute orientation, I need to have knowledge about the scene.

14:38.640 --> 14:40.920
I can't do that without.

14:40.920 --> 14:46.920
And this knowledge can, for example, be provided by the location of three 3D points in the

14:46.920 --> 14:47.920
world.

14:47.920 --> 14:52.280
If I have three 3D points in the world, I can compute the absolute orientation, estimate

14:52.280 --> 14:59.960
this similarity transform in order to put the photogrammetric model into the real world.

14:59.960 --> 15:04.920
So what is actually needed in order to estimate the full 3D model of the scene?

15:04.920 --> 15:12.160
We just learned three 3D points are given, but there are also other opportunities, options

15:12.160 --> 15:14.220
that I can have in order to do this.

15:14.220 --> 15:18.640
So consider you have two images, one image over here, one image over there, and you extract

15:18.640 --> 15:23.360
points in both images and you can triangulate those points, let's say an aerial image as

15:23.360 --> 15:24.880
is shown over here.

15:24.880 --> 15:29.360
So this is the part of the roof, it's identified in both images.

15:29.360 --> 15:31.680
This is one of those points over here.

15:31.680 --> 15:39.840
So what do we need to know in order to estimate where this point is in the real world?

15:39.840 --> 15:42.940
And then estimate where the cameras are in the world.

15:42.940 --> 15:51.760
So what I either need, I need to know three locations of points in the world.

15:51.760 --> 15:56.060
So if I would know the location of three different places in the world, I can actually build

15:56.060 --> 16:01.220
my photogrammetric model and then compute the absolute orientation of my photogrammetric

16:01.220 --> 16:02.220
model.

16:02.220 --> 16:09.380
What is also sufficient to know if I would know both camera locations, right, that's

16:09.380 --> 16:16.460
obvious if I know both camera locations with the heading information as well as the position

16:16.460 --> 16:22.380
information, I can just make a simple triangulation estimate where the point in the 3D world.

16:22.380 --> 16:26.380
Then assuming there are no errors involved, I can actually estimate the 3D location of

16:26.380 --> 16:28.340
the point directly.

16:28.340 --> 16:33.900
And what I also can do is I can actually estimate if I know the location of the camera number

16:33.900 --> 16:39.060
one only and I know the lengths of this vector here B or called baseline vector between the

16:39.060 --> 16:40.900
projection centers of the camera.

16:40.900 --> 16:46.780
I can also build the photogrammetric model from the two cameras and then locate it because

16:46.780 --> 16:50.900
this location gives me the six degrees of freedom that I don't know and the lengths

16:50.900 --> 16:55.540
of this vector gives me the seventh degree of freedom above the scale so that I then

16:55.540 --> 16:59.980
can recover a full 3D model about the world.

16:59.980 --> 17:06.180
Other things are if I know distances in the real world, then I can also use this information

17:06.180 --> 17:08.900
in order to build a 3D model.

17:08.900 --> 17:12.660
This was so far done for two calibrated cameras.

17:12.660 --> 17:17.020
So how does it look like if we are looking for uncalibrated cameras?

17:17.020 --> 17:22.860
So in uncalibrated cameras, I'm talking here about cameras without nonlinear errors.

17:22.860 --> 17:27.380
So we are only taking these 11 degrees of freedom per camera into account.

17:27.380 --> 17:31.740
So five for the calibration matrix and six for the extrinsics.

17:31.740 --> 17:36.400
And then we have a straight line preserving mapping but not an angle preserving mapping.

17:36.400 --> 17:40.000
So everything which is a straight line in the real world stays a straight line but the

17:40.000 --> 17:45.660
angles between straight lines are not preserved in our image.

17:45.660 --> 17:53.580
And so the object that I can reconstruct from that are also objects that I can be reconstructed

17:53.580 --> 17:55.920
up to a straight line preserving mapping.

17:55.920 --> 18:01.880
So that means I have a projective transform that I cannot resolve.

18:01.880 --> 18:06.960
A projective transform in 3D is a 15 degree of freedom transformation.

18:06.960 --> 18:11.560
So it's basically my homogeneous transformation matrix which is a 4 by 4 matrix.

18:11.560 --> 18:17.600
One parameter is gone for the scale of the homogeneity and so I have 15 parameters left

18:17.600 --> 18:20.520
and this is my projective transformation.

18:20.520 --> 18:30.080
That means from the 22 parameters that I have, 15 I cannot resolve.

18:30.080 --> 18:35.660
That means there are only seven parameters that I can actually resolve giving a pair

18:35.660 --> 18:37.060
of images.

18:37.060 --> 18:42.100
So for the uncalibrated camera I can only estimate seven parameters compared to the

18:42.100 --> 18:45.480
five parameters of the uncalibrated camera.

18:45.480 --> 18:49.320
That means in contrast if you want to compute the absolute orientation for a calibrated

18:49.320 --> 18:54.880
camera up here, we need to have at least five points in terms of 3D locations because then

18:54.880 --> 18:57.920
we have three times five equals 15.

18:57.920 --> 19:02.040
So the minimum number of control points that I need in order to create an absolute orientation

19:02.040 --> 19:09.400
for the uncalibrated camera, so under a projective transform in 3D is actually five.

19:09.400 --> 19:12.000
So minimum five points I need.

19:12.000 --> 19:18.100
This also means that if I have a pair of an uncalibrated camera, the maximum number of

19:18.100 --> 19:24.280
parameters I can estimate describing the relative orientation are those seven parameters in

19:24.280 --> 19:25.280
here.

19:25.280 --> 19:30.160
So the seven parameters I can actually extract from knowing just correspondences between

19:30.160 --> 19:35.100
image but not knowing anything about the scene itself.

19:35.100 --> 19:39.920
So in terms of the summary on this orientation, so you have the calibrated camera.

19:39.920 --> 19:45.180
Every camera has six parameters, just the extrinsics.

19:45.180 --> 19:47.600
So X, Y, Z, your control.

19:47.600 --> 19:54.140
You have the camera pair of twice the number, so six times two gives me 12.

19:54.140 --> 19:57.940
So we have the relative orientation, so what we can actually estimate without knowing any

19:57.940 --> 20:00.580
knowledge about the scene are those five parameters.

20:00.580 --> 20:04.900
This is kind of where is the second camera with respect to the first camera in terms

20:04.900 --> 20:11.100
of the orientation of three and two with respect to the direction vector, which is the direction

20:11.100 --> 20:12.300
vector of the projection centers.

20:12.300 --> 20:13.620
The lengths I can't estimate.

20:13.620 --> 20:17.400
Therefore, I have only five and not six parameters here.

20:17.400 --> 20:20.820
So we have remaining seven parameters for the absolute orientation.

20:20.820 --> 20:26.560
That means I need to have at least three 3D points in order to fix those seven parameters

20:26.560 --> 20:28.880
for computing the absolute orientation.

20:28.880 --> 20:35.700
For the uncalibrated camera, we have 11 parameters per camera, so 22 for the pair.

20:35.700 --> 20:41.300
We know everything up to a projective transform, so 15 parameters can't be fixed.

20:41.300 --> 20:46.180
Seven can be fixed just by looking to correspondences between images.

20:46.180 --> 20:52.800
So that we need five control points at minimum to fix the absolute orientation.

20:52.800 --> 21:03.000
So this first part allowed us to quantify what properties of the orientation are kind

21:03.000 --> 21:07.520
of relative, which I can just estimate between the two cameras, and what are absolute where

21:07.520 --> 21:10.440
I need external reference information.

21:10.440 --> 21:16.120
And so those numbers are very important information, and we will deal a lot in the subsequent part

21:16.460 --> 21:23.900
on how to estimate these five or seven parameters for the relative orientation.

21:23.900 --> 21:27.660
So relative orientation plays an important role because this is the information that

21:27.660 --> 21:30.420
you can actually estimate just from using a camera.

21:30.420 --> 21:35.340
If you take a camera and you move a camera through the world, you can actually best estimate

21:35.340 --> 21:40.100
the parameters of the relative orientation without knowing anything about the scene just

21:40.100 --> 21:43.340
from using your image information.

21:43.560 --> 21:47.320
In the end, this works by finding points in two images, which are identical, but I have

21:47.320 --> 21:49.720
no idea where those points are in the world.

21:49.720 --> 21:56.280
Just by knowing this point correspondence information, we can actually estimate or recover

21:56.280 --> 22:02.560
the motion of the camera, at least these five degrees of freedom for the calibrated camera

22:02.560 --> 22:06.240
and seven degrees of freedom for the uncalibrated camera.

22:07.140 --> 22:15.700
Okay, so let's look into the geometry a bit more and see how we can describe certain properties

22:15.700 --> 22:16.700
in there.

22:16.700 --> 22:19.840
This will lead us to the so-called fundamental matrix.

22:19.840 --> 22:23.580
So we are starting now with the uncalibrated camera, so assuming we have no calibration

22:23.580 --> 22:24.580
information.

22:24.580 --> 22:28.780
And we will look into something which is called the coplanarity constraint for the straight

22:28.780 --> 22:31.980
line preserving camera, that means for the uncalibrated camera.

22:32.720 --> 22:36.600
Okay, so let's again assume we don't know anything about the scene, and we want to start

22:36.600 --> 22:44.920
let's say having perfect orientation, so we don't have any issues that we don't see a

22:44.920 --> 22:48.140
point perfectly, so there's no noise in our measurement.

22:48.140 --> 22:51.660
And we have one camera which is sitting over here, and the second camera which is actually

22:51.660 --> 22:53.020
sitting over here.

22:53.020 --> 22:57.920
And they both observe a point x in the world.

22:57.920 --> 23:01.920
So the question, if I have this information, what do I know?

23:01.920 --> 23:06.540
I do know that the points end at exactly the same point in the world.

23:06.540 --> 23:11.980
So this is what I assumed was that everything is running perfectly here.

23:11.980 --> 23:13.820
So there's no noise in this process.

23:13.820 --> 23:19.260
So those directions vectors ought to go from here to here and here to here.

23:19.260 --> 23:22.860
I know they will intersect in the point x.

23:22.860 --> 23:27.980
So this means that the rays that I'm shooting from the individual projection center, from

23:27.980 --> 23:31.680
projection center of camera one and projection center from camera two, will meet in a point

23:31.740 --> 23:37.900
that means there's a plane in 3D where those two vectors lie within that plane.

23:37.900 --> 23:41.180
And that is something that I can actually exploit.

23:41.180 --> 23:46.300
If I know that those two vectors lie in a plane, I also know that the vector connecting

23:46.300 --> 23:51.100
the two projection centers will actually lie in a plane.

23:51.100 --> 23:55.580
So I have three vectors in 3D that lie in a plane.

23:56.440 --> 24:01.880
And this is something which I can express in this form over here.

24:01.880 --> 24:08.760
So what is written here is a scalar triple product which is equal to zero if all the

24:08.760 --> 24:12.400
vectors that I have in here lie in a plane in 3D.

24:12.400 --> 24:17.400
So I have once a vector which goes from O to x'.

24:17.400 --> 24:24.860
So this is the point O x' is the vector going from the projection center of the first camera

24:24.860 --> 24:26.280
to the point in 3D.

24:26.280 --> 24:28.420
So x O'.

24:28.420 --> 24:35.680
I have the vector going from O' to O'' this is the baseline vector I have here between

24:35.680 --> 24:36.960
the two cameras.

24:36.960 --> 24:41.400
So where it basically is from the projection center of camera one points to the projection

24:41.400 --> 24:48.920
center of camera number two, this one, and then from O'' to x'' whereas x' and x'' refer

24:48.920 --> 24:52.920
to the same point x in the 3D world.

24:52.980 --> 24:57.340
So this is a vector, this is a vector, this is a vector, and this is a vector, so they're

24:57.340 --> 25:00.520
not necessarily unit length.

25:00.520 --> 25:04.900
And then what this expression tells me is that the scalar triple product is equal to

25:04.900 --> 25:08.020
zero means that these points lie in a plane.

25:08.020 --> 25:13.340
So what is the scalar triple product, or in German, spartproduct?

25:13.340 --> 25:19.340
This is an operator which takes three vectors as an input and it can easily be defined by

25:19.340 --> 25:23.160
the cross product and the dot product.

25:23.160 --> 25:32.400
So it's basically the cross product A x B, the resulting vector with the scalar product

25:32.400 --> 25:34.480
with the vector C.

25:34.480 --> 25:40.860
And what it basically computes is the volume of the parallelepiped that is spent by the

25:40.860 --> 25:41.860
three vectors.

25:41.860 --> 25:49.860
So you have these three vectors, A, B, and C, and basically the volume that we have here.

25:49.860 --> 26:00.020
And you can also directly see that if the vector, so A x B, what it basically computes

26:00.020 --> 26:04.540
is a vector that is orthogonal on A and B.

26:04.540 --> 26:08.640
And so if I compute the dot product between this vector which is orthogonal on A and B

26:08.640 --> 26:14.660
where C is zero, that means C must lie in the plane spent by A and B.

26:14.660 --> 26:17.080
And this would mean this volume is zero.

26:17.080 --> 26:20.580
And this means that all three vectors lie in a plane.

26:20.580 --> 26:26.900
And this is exactly the result if A, B, C equals zero, that means that the vectors lie

26:26.900 --> 26:30.060
in one single plane in 3D.

26:30.060 --> 26:35.180
So what I've been doing here, I'm writing this constraint down, and this is something

26:35.180 --> 26:40.360
which I can write down without having any knowledge about the scene just by knowing

26:40.360 --> 26:47.860
the fact that both cameras observe the same point in the environment.

26:47.860 --> 26:58.120
So what we need to do now is to replace this O' X', O' O'' O'' X'' with entities that we

26:58.120 --> 27:03.920
know that relates that to the properties of our camera.

27:03.920 --> 27:05.700
So what do we know about the cameras?

27:05.700 --> 27:12.500
We know that those cameras project 3D points into our image.

27:12.500 --> 27:14.740
And this works with X equals PX.

27:14.740 --> 27:19.800
So again, every prime variable is related to camera one, the double prime variables

27:19.800 --> 27:22.120
are related to camera number two.

27:22.120 --> 27:27.020
But we have the same point X over here because we know it's the same point in the 3D world,

27:27.020 --> 27:29.900
although we do not know the 3D coordinate of that point in the world.

27:29.900 --> 27:33.100
But we know it's the same one, that's important.

27:33.100 --> 27:38.280
And then we know that the projection matrix of the camera number one is K'.

27:38.280 --> 27:42.560
So it's the calibration matrix of the first camera, the rotation of the first camera,

27:42.560 --> 27:46.220
and the translation vector of the first projection center, and the same thing for camera number

27:46.220 --> 27:47.220
two.

27:47.220 --> 27:52.160
So two cameras which have potentially a different projection vector.

27:52.160 --> 27:55.960
So this is how I can write down the projection matrices.

27:55.960 --> 28:02.080
Just as a short reminder, this notation over here means this is a 3x3 matrix, this is the

28:02.080 --> 28:03.760
identity matrix over here.

28:03.760 --> 28:12.480
And then we have the parameters for the translation vector in here in X, Y, and that coordinate.

28:12.480 --> 28:18.400
Okay, so now what we wanted to look is we actually wanted to look into those vectors.

28:18.400 --> 28:20.600
What are those vectors?

28:20.600 --> 28:28.180
And these are vectors which I can compute, taking into account the calibration matrix

28:28.180 --> 28:29.180
of our camera.

28:29.180 --> 28:36.620
If you remember correctly, the calibration matrix inverse multiplied with the pixel coordinate

28:36.620 --> 28:45.780
that I obtained, giving me the direction vector expressed in the coordinate system of my camera.

28:45.780 --> 28:50.500
So I need to now go slightly a step further and also put the orientation of camera one

28:50.500 --> 28:54.900
and orientation of camera two into account if I want to put this into a global reference

28:54.900 --> 28:58.700
frame or at least in the same reference frame of the two cameras.

28:58.700 --> 29:03.740
So because these are direction vectors, so if I would write down here, I have my image

29:03.740 --> 29:08.380
coordinate, I'll multiply from the left-hand side the calibration matrix inversed.

29:08.380 --> 29:12.820
This would express me the direction vector, but expressed in the coordinate system of

29:12.820 --> 29:14.720
my camera number one.

29:14.720 --> 29:20.340
And so I need to take the inverse of the rotation matrix into account, into let's call it a

29:20.340 --> 29:26.340
normalized projection vector so that it will be the same for both cameras if I do that

29:26.340 --> 29:30.420
for camera number one and camera number two.

29:30.420 --> 29:37.100
So then I know that no orientations are involved anymore, so that this point must be equal

29:37.100 --> 29:48.220
just to the translation and the ideal projection that is generated by having my camera at the

29:48.220 --> 29:55.480
location of my projection center of camera one and multiplied with the 3D point in world

29:55.480 --> 29:57.700
coordinates.

29:57.700 --> 30:07.420
So that we obtain the point projected from this point, we can actually compute the direction

30:07.420 --> 30:10.640
vectors that we have in this normalized projection.

30:10.640 --> 30:16.200
And we can do analogously exactly the same thing for the second camera where I put the

30:16.200 --> 30:19.520
pixel coordinate of the second camera in there, take the calibration matrix of the

30:19.520 --> 30:24.340
second camera and the rotation matrix of the second camera, both inverted, and I obtain

30:24.340 --> 30:27.960
the same direction vector for the second camera.

30:27.960 --> 30:35.840
And this gives me this O prime X prime and O double prime X double prime, the direction

30:35.840 --> 30:42.720
vectors for my cameras, and then I have two of my vectors in my triangle already defined.

30:42.720 --> 30:48.660
And the same I can do with my baseline vector, so my baseline vector is just given by the

30:48.660 --> 30:50.940
difference of the true projection center.

30:50.940 --> 30:59.200
So this is the direction from one camera projection center to the second projection center.

30:59.200 --> 31:04.600
So what I now can do is I can take those expressions, so the baseline as well as those elements

31:04.600 --> 31:09.440
here and can put them into my original equation.

31:09.440 --> 31:16.880
So here again I had my scalar triple product which resulted from the coplanarity constraint,

31:16.880 --> 31:24.440
my three vectors, so this gives me the direction vector of the first camera shooting towards

31:24.440 --> 31:26.320
the corresponding point.

31:26.320 --> 31:34.880
This is my baseline vector and this over here is my normalized direction vector for the

31:34.880 --> 31:35.880
second camera.

31:35.880 --> 31:39.040
And I know that three elements should be zero.

31:39.040 --> 31:43.640
So I can take the definition from the triple scalar product, so I have the cross product

31:43.640 --> 31:56.440
between two vectors and the scalar product with the third vector, it should be zero.

31:56.440 --> 32:05.200
And what I then can do, I can rewrite this so that B cross product X can be written with

32:05.200 --> 32:06.660
a skew symmetric matrix.

32:06.660 --> 32:11.560
So again a skew symmetric matrix is a matrix which has a special form which I can use to

32:11.560 --> 32:18.500
express the cross product through a matrix vector multiplication.

32:18.500 --> 32:25.900
So nothing changes in here, it's just a different way for writing B with a cross product.

32:25.900 --> 32:30.200
So just to kind of illustrate you briefly that this is actually correct in case you

32:30.200 --> 32:33.300
didn't know how this skew symmetric matrix work.

32:33.300 --> 32:36.380
So the question is why is this correct?

32:36.380 --> 32:41.820
And then we can simply show this if I have vector B1 and I multiply it with a vector

32:41.820 --> 32:44.900
X with a cross product.

32:44.900 --> 32:53.260
So what I obtain, again if I have the cross product, so for the first coordinate I get

32:53.260 --> 33:02.760
my B3 times X2 and B3 times X2, just check for the correct signs that I have over here.

33:02.760 --> 33:11.920
And for the second coordinates it's X1B3 minus B1X3, which is over here.

33:11.920 --> 33:22.960
And for the third part I take X1 times B2 minus B1 times X2 plus, sorry, so this is

33:22.960 --> 33:24.640
the corresponding expression.

33:24.640 --> 33:31.840
And I get exactly the same result if I use a skew symmetric matrix which has B1 sitting

33:31.840 --> 33:40.060
over here, minus B2 here, B3 over here, 0 is in the main diagonal, and I multiply this

33:40.060 --> 33:41.560
matrix with this vector.

33:41.560 --> 33:47.660
So just to see that the first line is correct, what would happen, I multiply this vector

33:47.660 --> 33:53.840
over here with this vector over here, so 0 times X1, no X1 pops up here, minus B3 times

33:53.840 --> 34:01.840
X2, minus B3 times X2, plus B2 times X3, plus B2 times X3, gives me this expression.

34:01.840 --> 34:05.440
And I can repeat the same thing for the two other lines.

34:05.440 --> 34:16.040
So a skew symmetric matrix is always a matrix which has zeros on its main diagonal and the

34:16.040 --> 34:20.840
elements down here are the negatives of those elements up here.

34:20.840 --> 34:27.800
And so it's a matrix which is equivalent to the negative of its transposed.

34:27.800 --> 34:33.480
And this can be used to turn this cross product into a matrix vector product, which is quite

34:33.480 --> 34:38.280
nice because then I can actually write down things as a chain of matrix multiplications

34:38.280 --> 34:43.740
and do not have a cross product in between, although it is actually equivalent.

34:43.740 --> 34:50.820
So what I can do is, just to go back, I can actually write this expression in this form

34:50.820 --> 34:51.820
over here.

34:51.820 --> 34:59.540
So I turn this triple-spark product into a vector matrix vector equals to zero equation.

34:59.540 --> 35:04.980
Now let's take this equation here and continue with this equation.

35:04.980 --> 35:13.420
So what I now can do is I can again fill this normalized projection vector and put in my

35:13.420 --> 35:17.500
rotation matrix and my calibration matrix and the pixel coordinate in again, right?

35:17.500 --> 35:25.460
So I can take this expression and put it over here and could do the same thing for the second

35:25.460 --> 35:26.600
coordinate.

35:26.600 --> 35:32.820
So it's just expanded this expression, so it's X' transposed.

35:32.820 --> 35:37.020
So this is the actual pixel coordinate that I have.

35:37.020 --> 35:42.360
The inverse transposed of the calibration matrix of camera one, the inverse transposed

35:42.360 --> 35:47.520
of the rotation matrix of my camera one, which is actually the same in the rotation

35:47.520 --> 35:51.800
matrix of the camera one because I inverted and transposed it as long as it's a rotation

35:51.800 --> 35:52.800
matrix.

35:52.800 --> 35:55.660
The result is the same.

35:55.660 --> 35:57.680
Then I have my square symmetric matrix.

35:57.680 --> 36:03.680
I have my rotation matrix of camera two inverted, the calibration matrix of camera two inverted

36:03.680 --> 36:09.900
times the pixel coordinate of my camera number two, and this should be equal to zero.

36:09.900 --> 36:15.380
And now what I'm doing, I'm taking this whole thing over here in the middle.

36:15.380 --> 36:17.380
This is a matrix.

36:17.380 --> 36:22.580
And this is a matrix which contains a product of five matrices here, but I can condense

36:22.580 --> 36:23.580
them together.

36:23.580 --> 36:27.660
So it's a three by three matrix that results from this, and I simply give this matrix the

36:27.660 --> 36:29.260
name F.

36:29.260 --> 36:35.600
So this thing is subsidized in F so that F has the following form, and I can simplify

36:35.600 --> 36:40.800
that a little bit so that for the rotation matrix, the inverse transposed, so again inverse

36:40.800 --> 36:46.060
is equal to the transpose, and if I transpose the matrix twice, I get the original matrix.

36:46.060 --> 36:50.580
So this is just the rotation matrix, and R inverse is equal to R transposed.

36:50.580 --> 36:55.460
So this is my resulting definition of my matrix F.

36:55.460 --> 37:00.080
And this matrix F is the so-called fundamental matrix.

37:00.080 --> 37:06.640
And the fundamental matrix of this form describes all the parameters that we can actually estimate

37:06.640 --> 37:11.960
for the relative orientation of one camera with respect to another camera.

37:11.960 --> 37:16.420
And the elegant thing about this is that we can express the coplanarity constraint so

37:16.420 --> 37:21.160
that the constraint that all those vectors align the same plane can be written down as

37:21.160 --> 37:26.020
X prime transposed F X double prime equals to zero.

37:26.020 --> 37:36.340
So that means if I have two pixel coordinates of points which refers to the same point in

37:36.340 --> 37:45.380
the real world, I know that X transposed times the fundamental matrix times X double prime

37:45.380 --> 37:46.620
must give me zero.

37:46.620 --> 37:52.920
So this must be zero if that point is actually the same point in the real world.

37:52.920 --> 37:58.120
And this is important information that we're going to exploit several times in the remaining

37:58.120 --> 38:00.380
part of that course here.

38:00.380 --> 38:05.840
So the fundamental matrix is a matrix that fulfills this equation for all corresponding points.

38:05.840 --> 38:10.440
Whenever I have corresponding points in the image, this equation must hold.

38:10.440 --> 38:19.680
Of course, under the assumption that I have estimated those points perfectly so that there's

38:19.680 --> 38:20.680
no noise in the process.

38:20.880 --> 38:25.280
So in reality, you may not bring it down to zero but to small values.

38:25.280 --> 38:29.160
Just to keep that in mind, this assumes that we can perfectly measure the coordinates of

38:29.160 --> 38:31.360
all points.

38:31.360 --> 38:35.240
And so what is also worth mentioning is that the fundamental matrix is the matrix that

38:35.240 --> 38:41.080
contains all the information about the relative orientation of two images from an uncalibrated

38:41.080 --> 38:42.080
camera.

38:42.080 --> 38:45.880
So this means it has this seven degrees of freedom.

38:45.880 --> 38:53.360
So seven parameters are in there, seven free parameters, because it was 22 minus 15, which

38:53.360 --> 39:00.120
are gone for the projective transform, seven remaining, and so this matrix F has seven

39:00.120 --> 39:03.320
degrees of freedom.

39:03.320 --> 39:10.040
So the question is now, once we have our projection matrices, can we actually compute the fundamental

39:10.040 --> 39:12.280
matrix directly?

39:12.280 --> 39:18.160
So if we know the calibration matrix, the rotation matrix, and the translation vector,

39:18.160 --> 39:23.320
obviously we can directly compute the fundamental matrix just by building up the individual

39:23.320 --> 39:25.840
matrices and multiplying them with each other.

39:25.840 --> 39:30.840
But the question is now, can I also go from the projection matrices directly to the fundamental

39:30.840 --> 39:31.840
matrix?

39:31.840 --> 39:37.820
Just as a reminder, these projection matrices where these four by three matrices describing

39:37.820 --> 39:44.260
the mapping X equals PX, and assuming we have P for both cameras, can we compute the

39:44.260 --> 39:49.020
fundamental matrix, so the relative orientation between the two cameras?

39:49.020 --> 39:53.540
And we can do this, we can actually do this in a way which is related to how we did this

39:53.540 --> 40:01.060
in the direct linear transform when we were going back and trying to extract the decomposing

40:01.060 --> 40:05.220
rotation matrix and translation matrix, so there's some similarity in here, but in some

40:05.220 --> 40:06.860
it's actually easier.

40:06.860 --> 40:13.060
So the matrix P is a four by three matrix consisting of one three by three matrix A

40:13.060 --> 40:18.500
and a three by one vector, because it's three by four in sum.

40:18.500 --> 40:26.620
So what we have is this matrix P consisting of A, matrix A and the vector A, you can directly

40:26.620 --> 40:31.060
see that this three by three block over here is my matrix A, consists of the calibration

40:31.060 --> 40:35.580
matrix times the rotation matrix, and for the vector it's a negative calibration matrix

40:35.580 --> 40:41.580
times the rotation matrix times the projection center of my camera.

40:41.580 --> 40:45.980
And now if we look to this part over here, we see this matrix popping up over here, and

40:45.980 --> 40:48.280
this matrix being here, the same.

40:48.280 --> 40:53.940
So this is the matrix A, so if we invert this part, we can multiply from the left hand side

40:53.940 --> 40:58.020
and directly get the projection center of my camera.

40:58.020 --> 40:59.020
That's what I'm doing.

40:59.020 --> 41:02.860
I'm taking this matrix, I'm inverting this three by three matrix and multiplying it from

41:02.860 --> 41:04.900
left hand side to this equation.

41:04.900 --> 41:12.240
You can see over here, A inverse, A prime gives me K R inverse times A, which is nothing

41:12.240 --> 41:17.660
else than minus R transposed K inverse K R X zero.

41:17.660 --> 41:24.420
And so K inverse K becomes the identity, R transposed R becomes the identity, so minus

41:24.420 --> 41:26.900
X O remains.

41:26.900 --> 41:34.460
So that means the projection center of one of the cameras is simply given by minus A

41:34.460 --> 41:40.420
prime inverse A. It can do exactly the same thing for my camera number two, and then I

41:40.420 --> 41:45.880
know that the vector B, the baseline vector, is simply given by the difference between

41:45.880 --> 41:47.080
the two projection centers.

41:47.080 --> 41:53.420
So the vector from camera one to camera two is camera two minus the projection center

41:53.420 --> 41:55.060
of camera number one.

41:55.060 --> 41:57.820
So this is done in a straightforward manner.

41:57.820 --> 42:01.620
So this gives me my baseline vector.

42:01.620 --> 42:12.580
And through A, I have K times R, and through this expression, I have the vector B, and

42:12.580 --> 42:15.440
this is enough to build up my essential matrix.

42:15.440 --> 42:21.620
So we have A given, we have B given, and this is my fundamental matrix, so I can just write

42:21.620 --> 42:27.700
it down as A inverse transposed times the skew symmetric matrix, which I generate from

42:27.700 --> 42:35.460
this vector B, times A double prime inverse, with my skew symmetric matrix as defined here.

42:35.460 --> 42:41.900
So I can directly compute the fundamental matrix based on the two projection matrices

42:41.900 --> 42:43.580
that I have.

42:44.540 --> 42:48.260
There's one thing I should note.

42:48.260 --> 42:58.900
There's a second definition of the fundamental matrix, which yields a constraint where everything

42:58.900 --> 42:59.900
is transposed.

42:59.900 --> 43:06.060
So if you look to our coplanarity constraint as we defined it here, we could also transpose

43:06.060 --> 43:07.820
both equations.

43:07.820 --> 43:13.620
So zero transposed gives zero, and I transpose the whole block over here.

43:13.620 --> 43:23.360
So we would then get X double prime transposed, T transposed, X prime equals to zero.

43:23.360 --> 43:29.140
And this is an alternative definition, which is basically the fundamental matrix there

43:29.140 --> 43:32.380
is the transposed of the fundamental matrix.

43:32.380 --> 43:37.460
So especially if we have multiple camera systems and we compute the fundamental matrix from

43:37.500 --> 43:46.980
a camera ij, this is, I could generate a second fundamental matrix, which is fji transposed.

43:46.980 --> 43:50.340
And so that means there are two different definitions of the fundamental matrix which

43:50.340 --> 43:51.460
are out there.

43:51.460 --> 43:56.780
So the one that we used is basically f12 from camera one, camera two, and this is a standard

43:56.780 --> 43:58.860
one used in photogrammetry.

