to zero if we can express everything directly in the camera coordinate system on the camera

48:34.080 --> 48:35.660
frame.

48:35.660 --> 48:40.240
So the essential matrix is nothing else than a special form of fundamental matrix, basically

48:40.240 --> 48:43.520
fundamental matrix for the calibrated camera.

48:43.520 --> 48:50.140
And then things get easier because the calibration matrix is basically go away and just the rotation

48:50.140 --> 48:56.260
matrix and the screw symmetric matrix and the second rotation matrix actually survives

48:56.260 --> 48:57.260
this process.

48:57.260 --> 49:04.120
And we can see here already that it only involves the rotational parameters and it only involves

49:04.120 --> 49:05.900
the baseline parameters.

49:05.900 --> 49:11.100
So in the end this will be a matrix which has five degrees of freedom because these

49:11.100 --> 49:18.780
are the five elements we can actually estimate just based on camera images because we cannot

49:18.780 --> 49:23.980
determine the similarity transform for the absolute orientation, so the remaining relative

49:23.980 --> 49:28.300
one are the five degrees of freedom which will be encoded in this matrix E which is

49:28.300 --> 49:34.180
again a three by three matrix but only has five degrees of freedom in here.

49:34.180 --> 49:39.380
And we can use it in exactly the same way as we used it with the fundamental matrix

49:39.380 --> 49:45.020
to describe the coplanarity constraint, we use the essential matrix for the calibrated

49:45.020 --> 49:46.020
