51:34.300 --> 51:40.940
And this depends on the parameterization how in which I describe how camera two is related

51:40.940 --> 51:43.460
with respect to camera one.

51:43.460 --> 51:49.380
And we are basically looking in here to three popular parameterizations which are out there

51:49.380 --> 51:53.140
to describe these five degrees of freedom.

51:53.140 --> 51:59.180
So there are ways where one puts, or what all approaches do, they put the first camera

51:59.180 --> 52:03.060
into the center of the reference frame and then describe the second camera with respect

52:03.060 --> 52:04.580
to the first camera.

52:04.580 --> 52:10.420
This can be done by saying either this is, can be described by a rotation matrix how

52:10.420 --> 52:16.020
camera two is basically looking with respect to camera number one and our baseline vector

52:16.020 --> 52:21.380
and then the baseline vector is restricted to a certain length, for example unit lengths,

52:21.380 --> 52:31.020
or other approaches which are more traditional ones use on this baseline vector set one parameter

52:31.020 --> 52:35.140
to a constant value so that only two degrees of freedom are left for example the y and

52:35.140 --> 52:40.060
z coordinate or even a transformation which expresses the whole transformation only in

52:40.060 --> 52:41.060
