52:41.060 --> 52:48.660
And this kind of underlies in a nice way that the camera is actually an orientation measurement

52:48.660 --> 52:53.380
device and that we can actually express everything with respect to orientations.

52:53.380 --> 52:57.100
So again the first one, this one over here is so called the general parameterization

52:57.100 --> 53:01.060
of dependent images and is by far the most frequently used one.

53:01.060 --> 53:06.780
So by today this is typically the parameterization of your choice that you're typically taking.

53:06.780 --> 53:10.380
Then there's the so called photogrammetric parameterization of dependent images.

53:10.380 --> 53:17.040
This is the one which sets one parameter to zero so the bx, to a constant so this basically

53:17.040 --> 53:23.920
bx, the offset in the x direction which can lead to some mathematical problems especially

53:23.920 --> 53:28.600
if you have zero movement along this direction between the two cameras.

53:28.600 --> 53:33.080
And then there's the parameterization with independent images which doesn't take any

53:33.080 --> 53:37.360
